WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Time to talk about Benchmarks — the leaderboard scores you have probably seen elsewhere, and why the app hands them to you for free but does not want you stopping there.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.082
Open Library, Benchmarks.

3
00:00:08.082 --> 00:00:16.000
It is a table of public leaderboard scores — coding, reasoning, general knowledge — pulled in for every model the app knows about, so you can sort and compare without leaving the app.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.682
This is the one thing the app fetches for you instead of routing through your own provider key.

5
00:00:19.682 --> 00:00:26.000
A small gateway serves the scores; it needs no key of yours, and it carries no data about you or your machine — just public numbers matched against the model list.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:26.776
Here is the catch.

7
00:00:26.776 --> 00:00:29.839
These test questions are, at this point, plastered across the internet.

8
00:00:29.839 --> 00:00:37.000
Training data for newer models likely includes material that looks a lot like the benchmark itself — so a high score partly reflects memorization, not raw capability.

9
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.426
Which makes a benchmark a weak proxy for your actual task.

10
00:00:39.426 --> 00:00:44.110
A model that tops a coding leaderboard can still fumble the exact tool-calling pattern your workflow depends on.

11
00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:48.000
Use benchmarks to narrow the field — three or four candidates — never to make the final call.

12
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.288
So the rule: shortlist with Benchmarks, then test with Evals — your own test cases, on your own task.

13
00:00:53.288 --> 00:00:58.000
That is next episode, and it is the step that actually tells you which model wins for you.

14
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.045
So how do you build a test that actually reflects your work?

15
00:01:00.045 --> 00:01:04.000
Next episode we build a real eval set — a handful of test cases with a right answer, run straight against any model.

